parsed_suggested_fix:
Split the class by responsibility and make each service own a single business capability. Extract validation and rule evaluation into focused components, and replace string status codes with typed results or enums to make behavior explicit.

Concrete steps:
1. Extract count recording/variance logic into a CountService.
2. Extract reconciliation/discrepancy rules into a ReconciliationService.
3. Extract valuation logic into a ValuationService.
4. Move shared state behind dedicated repositories or state objects instead of keeping unrelated dictionaries/lists in one class.
5. Introduce small result types or enums instead of returning magic strings like "match", "zeroed", or "phantom".
6. Centralize repeated input validation (for example SKU validation) in helper methods or value objects.

Example refactoring:
```csharp
public enum CountStatus { NoSku, InvalidCount, NoWarehouse, Match, LargeVariance, Zeroed, Recorded }

public sealed class CycleCountRecorder
{
    private readonly IDictionary<string, int> _counts;
    private readonly IValuationService _valuationService;

    public CycleCountRecorder(IDictionary<string, int> counts, IValuationService valuationService)
    {
        _counts = counts;
        _valuationService = valuationService;
    }

    public CountStatus RecordCount(StockItem item, Warehouse warehouse, int counted)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(item.Sku)) return CountStatus.NoSku;
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(warehouse.Code)) return CountStatus.NoWarehouse;
        if (counted < 0) return CountStatus.InvalidCount;

        _counts[item.Sku] = counted;
        var variance = counted - item.OnHand;

        if (variance != 0)
            _valuationService.UpdateVarianceImpact(item.Sku, variance);

        if (variance == 0) return CountStatus.Match;
        if (variance > 100 || variance < -100) return CountStatus.LargeVariance;
        if (counted == 0 && item.OnHand > 0) return CountStatus.Zeroed;
        return CountStatus.Recorded;
    }
}
```
This decomposition reduces complexity per class, isolates side effects, and allows each rule set to be tested independently.
